he experiments directory. So, the code would be:\n\nIn experiments/solution.py:\n\ndef search(arr):\n    result = 0\n    for num in arr:\n        result ^= num\n    return result\n\nBut the user may have other requirements, but based on the problem, this is the correct approach.\n\nSo, the final answer is the code for the function, and the test file.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of identifying the unique integer in a sorted array of integers where all other elements are paired, we can utilize the **XOR** bitwise operation. This operation is efficient and works as follows:\n\n- The XOR of a number with itself is 0.\n- The XOR of a number with 0 is the number itself.\n\nThus, if we XOR all the numbers in the array, the result is the unique number, because all the duplicate numbers will cancel out (i.e., XOR with themselves results in 0).\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Solution\n\n```python\ndef search(arr):\n    result = 0\n    for num in arr:\n        result ^= num\n    return result\n```\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Unit Tests\n\nWe also need to provide unit tests for this function to ensure correctness. These tests can be placed in a file like `experiments/test_search.py`.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\nclass TestSearch(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_search(self):\n        self.assertEqual(search([1, 1, 2, 2, 3]), 3)\n        self.assertEqual(search([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4]), 1)\n        self.assertEqual(search([5, 5, 5, 5, 5]), 5)\n        self.assertEqual(search([10, 20, 30, 40, 50]), 10)\n        self.assertEqual(search([1, 1, 1, 1, 1]), 1)\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 📁 File Structure\n\n- `experiments/solution.py`: Contains the `search` function.\n- `experiments/test_search.py`: Contains the unit tests.\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Implementation Notes\n\n- This approach is efficient with **O(n)** time complexity, where `n` is the length of the array.\n- The code'
